  "query_preview": "..\nSynoptic Setup:\nA weak ridge is over the western Great Plains and a closed upper-\nlevel low has moved over the Pacific Northwest. The interaction \nof these two systems has produced SW flow aloft over Montana, \ntransporting Pacific moisture into the region. \n\nToday Through Tomorrow: \nWhile the stability in the upper atmosphere will keep conditions\ndry into early this evening, the aforementioned PNW low will\nweaken and dig over Montana tonight. This will likely produce\nscattered rain showers and embedded thunderstorms beginning around\nmidnight and lasting through tomorrow afternoon. A cold front\nassociated with the trough will likely pass between midnight and\n6AM, producing precipitation totals between 0.01\" and 0.50\".\nAdditionally, vorticity-indudced instability associated with the\nbackside of the low may result in rain showers lasting into early\nMonday evening across northern portions of the CWA. Confidence in\nprecipitation amounts associated with later afternoon and evening\nshowers is low, but expect higher totals north of a line drawn \nbetween Malta and Terry.\n\nWinds will veer and become westerly behind the front Monday \nmorning and be comparable in strength to winds experienced today.\nWinds will reach Lake Wind Advisory Criteria tomorrow. Wave \nheights will have the potential to reach 4 to 5 ft tomorrow \nafternoon.\n\nTuesday Onward:\nThe trough will fully exit the region by Tuesday morning, leaving\nzonal flow in its wake. Slight chances for rain showers are\npossible Tuesday night into Wednesday morning due to a swath of\nPacific moisture moving throughout the flow. Confidence in rain\namounts is low, but best chances will be across northern portions\nof the CWA. Rain chances fully diminish by Wednesday afternoon as\nskies clear and temperatures warm under a developing ridge over \nthe Central US. Although, a weak trough has embedded itself \nbetween us and the ridge in a significant portion of the \nensembles, which could potentially produce rain and increasing \ncloud coverage Friday night.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nThere is HIGH confidence in sustained winds at or above 20 mph on\ntoday and tomorrow across Fort Peck Lake.\n\nThere is MODERATE confidence in the timing of scattered showers \nand thunderstorms on tonight into Monday (They could begin \nearlier or later), but there HIGH confidence in them lasting into\nthe evenings hours.\n\nThere is LOW confidence in the forecast beyond Thursday.\n\nDON",
